From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justinas Grauslis <justinas@8devices.com>
Date: Thu, 18 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] detect emmc from the unsuffixed xbl

set_active_boot_slot() decides whether the storage is eMMC by resolving
"xbl_a", then refuses to continue when that says UFS and /dev/bsg/ufs-bsg0
cannot be opened:

  Unable to open '/dev/bsg/ufs-bsg0': No such file or directory
  Is CONFIG_SCSI_UFS_BSG is enabled in your kernel?
  SLOT b: Failed to set active

A board whose xbl carries no slot suffix has no xbl_a to resolve, so the
lookup fails and gpt_utils_is_partition_backed_by_emmc() reports false, which
the caller reads as UFS. The kernel option named in the message is a red
herring: an eMMC board has no UFS controller to expose a bsg node in the first
place. Reads are unaffected, so only setting a slot fails, and with it every
A/B update and rollback.

Fall back to the unsuffixed partition name. Boards that do have xbl_a resolve
it first and are unchanged; the rest now report eMMC correctly, which also
skips the xbl boot-partition switch they have no use for.

Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 bootctrl_impl.c | 5 ++++-
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/bootctrl_impl.c b/bootctrl_impl.c
--- a/bootctrl_impl.c
+++ b/bootctrl_impl.c
@@ -705,7 +705,10 @@
 		return -1;
 	}
 
-	ismmc = gpt_utils_is_partition_backed_by_emmc(PTN_XBL AB_SLOT_A_SUFFIX);
+	// An unsuffixed xbl leaves xbl_a unresolvable, which the lookup reports
+	// as UFS. Fall back to the plain name before believing that.
+	ismmc = gpt_utils_is_partition_backed_by_emmc(PTN_XBL AB_SLOT_A_SUFFIX) ||
+		gpt_utils_is_partition_backed_by_emmc(PTN_XBL);
 
 	// Do this *before* updating all the slot attributes
 	// to make sure we can
